ll,"result":"I'm approving PKT-0015. The goal is met with the defect/crash rate moved out, the contract is kept, and the budget holds (6 of 6 files, about 408 changed lines of the 800 allowed).\n\n**Goal**\n- `baseline.md` now has p50, p99 and throughput for ConvertService (7 format pairs, with 1 and 4 parallel clients), plus raw x2t times for comparison. It also has open, join, co-edit round trip and save, measured with 2 and 4 clients on the full stack.\n- The upstream defect/crash rate is not measured. Moving it to `.theseus/spawn/PKT-0015-defect-and-crash-rate-from-upstream-history.md` was one of the two options the last review offered. The Goal paragraph, the \"NOT MEASURED\" section and the gaps table all point to that request. The second follow-up (split the co-edit tail and load-test) is also in `.theseus/spawn/`. The \"Spawned packets\" section is empty, but having the requests in `.theseus/spawn/` meets the TEP-0004 rule.\n\n**Contract**\n- No project source changed. The only workarounds were runtime changes inside the container (`local.json`), and both the log and `baseline.md` record them.\n\n**Previous rejections**\n- The Executor log now describes the files as they are: samples come from `$SAMPLES`, and the x2t loop is a code block in `baseline.md`. It also notes that the executor edited the Goal paragraph because the review asked for it.\n\n**Measurement code**\n- p99 is computed in a way that makes it the maximum at these sample sizes (20 conversions, or 100 to 180 round trips), and the tables say so for ConvertService.\n- The co-edit script filters out failed samples (NaN) and reports how many there were.\n- Edits are sequential with a warm-up edit, so the numbers are latency, not load. `baseline.md` says this.\n- The co-edit tail is not attributed to the server. It is left as a hypothesis with a follow-up request, which is the right call.\n\n**Minor, not blocking**\n- The header comment of `perf/e2e-convert.js` says it times ConvertService in sync 